</div>

<div class="callout callout-info">
  <strong>SSO Constraint Note:</strong> Sun-synchronous orbits (inclination ~97°–99°) occupy a narrow inclination band.
  The finite number of usable nodal precession rates makes this a <strong>congestion-sensitive resource</strong> — unlike
  the wider mid-inclination LEO bands where Starlink operates dozens of planes at common altitudes.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 6: CONCLUSIONS ═══════════ -->
<section>
<h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">06</span>Key Takeaways &amp; Outlook</h2>

<div class="narrative">
  <strong>The orbital environment has entered a new era.</strong> The 2026 active-satellite catalog reveals a domain
  transformed by a single operator, a single orbital regime, and a five-year launch surge without historical precedent.
</div>

<ol style="margin: 0 0 20px 20px; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.8;">
  <li>
    <strong>LEO is the new center of gravity.</strong> At """ + f"{leo_pct:.1f}% of all active satellites, LEO" + """
    hosts the vast majority of the operational fleet. This concentration demands updated approaches
    to conjunction assessment, debris mitigation, and traffic coordination — especially given the
    altitude overlap between mega-constellations and legacy science/defense missions.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Starlink's dominance reshapes every statistical picture.</strong> With """ + f"{starlink_count:,}" + """ satellites
    (""" + f"{starlink_count/total_sats*100:.1f}% of the fleet), Starlink alone " + """is larger than the entire combined
    catalog from any prior decade. Any policy framework that does not account for single-operator
    concentration risks will be incomplete.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The launch cadence is still accelerating.</strong> """ + f"{post_2020_pct:.0f}%" + """ of currently active satellites
    were launched after 2020. If current trends continue, the catalog could exceed 25,000–30,000 active
    objects by decade's end — most of them in a few narrow altitude shells in LEO.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Orbital niches remain viable.</strong> Despite LEO dominance, GEO persists as the platform for
    high-throughput broadcast and weather services, while MEO hosts critical GNSS infrastructure
    (GPS, Galileo, BeiDou). HEO serves specialized high-latitude communications roles.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Spectrum and debris policy must evolve.</strong> The density of active satellites at common altitudes
    and inclinations means that spectrum sharing, collision avoidance, and post-mission disposal are
    no longer edge cases — they are operational necessities for every constellation operator.
  </li>
</ol>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
  <strong>For Policymakers:</strong> Consider introducing <strong>altitude-band occupancy limits</strong>
  and <strong>aggregate collision-probability budgets</strong> for orbital shells above a certain
  object density. The current per-satellite safety framework does not scale to a post-Starlink
  environment where a single operator can deploy thousands of spacecraft in coordinated planes.
</div>
</section>

</div><!-- /container -->

<footer>
  Report generated from the CelesTrak active-satellite catalog (""" + f"{total_sats:,}" + """ objects) via NORAD TLE data.<br>
  Analysis: orbit classification by MEAN_MOTION thresholds; constellation parsing by OBJECT_NAME prefix matching.<br>
  All figures and statistics derived directly from the source dataset — no fabricated or estimated values.
</footer>

</body>
</html>"""

